.SH BUGS
.BR wait ,
.BR send ,
.B recv
and
.B bind
are refused; process, IPC and network requests belong to the user-level
services described in the Osprey plan.
.PP
Servicing is driven by writes to
.BR ctl ,
and the dispatcher runs in the namespace of whichever process wrote
.BR service .
Scheduling is driven by the clock, but a container is not yet attached to
a
.IR Proc ,
so exhausting a budget throttles the container's place in the Osprey run
queue and not the process behind it.
.PP
Network accounting has no packet path charging it yet; the caps and
counters are there for the one the plan adds later.
.PP
The whole span is mapped by any process that attaches it, so one
container can read and write another's rings, and the kernel address in
each ring head is visible to them all.
.PP
A ring carries one producer and one consumer.  Two processes submitting
to the same container, or a process submitting while another writes
.B submit
to
.B ctl
for it, will corrupt the ring; the device sleeps one waiter per container
per direction and does not arbitrate between more.
